Notebook[{
Cell["Benchmark: Kuramoto-Sivashinsky at order p^4", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"d7bc2eb1-f11c-48e9-a779-e227415f79bb"],

Cell["\<\
Reference: L. Gosteva, N. Wschebor, L. Canet, \"Scaling regimes of the \
Kuramoto-Sivashinsky equation from the functional renormalization group\", \
arXiv:2607.15784 (2026), Sec. III (simplest derivative-expansion ansatz). The \
paper gives the one-loop flows of its four running couplings for general \
regulators, after the frequency integral (Eqs. 34-37). This notebook derives \
the same flows with FRGscalarMSR from the ansatz alone and compares them with \
the paper's, exactly, at every loop momentum.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"69971a47-26f6-4023-935e-39f71195979d"],

Cell["\<\
Why this benchmark: tau is the coefficient of p^4 in Gamma(1,1), so its flow \
tests the momentum projection at order p^4 (n = 2), which no other literature \
check reaches. Two exact identities of the paper are checked too: the \
cancellation of r''' in the tau flow, and the time-reversal symmetry of the \
one-dimensional flows. Limit: at d = 1 the p^4 normalisation 8d(d+2) equals \
8(d^2+2), so a d-normalisation error would not show here \
(Projection.normalisation and Projection.explicitVectors cover it).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"35f62527-4677-4529-a449-5ccadb0deb2d"],

Cell["\<\
Get[\"~/Physics/Sources/FRGscalarMSR/FRGscalarMSR.wl\"];
ksDir = ExpandFileName[\"~/Physics/Sources/FRGscalarMSR/benchmarks/\
KuramotoSivashinsky\"];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"4f160eda-24e3-4313-9fab-fb74c3932d6d"],

Cell["1. Model and ansatz (paper Eqs. 16-17)", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"cc2ad202-5568-442b-82e0-b71e92d1b9c4"],

Cell["\<\
Gamma_k = Int h\:0304 { dt h - (nu - tau dx^2) dx^2 h - lam/2 (dx h)^2 - (D - \
zeta dx^2) h\:0304 }, so Gamma(1,1) = i w + p^2 (nu + tau p^2) and Gamma(0,2) \
= -2 (D + zeta p^2). The term zeta (dx h\:0304)^2 is zero at the KS scale and \
generated by the flow. lam and the 1 in front of dt h are not renormalised \
(Galilean and shift symmetries). The dimension is kept symbolic (Dim) and set \
to 1 in the comparisons, which lets the time-reversal check use d = 2 as a \
control.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e5223cc4-9037-4dcd-8853-676d30bb6a82"],

Cell["\<\
ksTheory = DefineTheory[\"Scalars\" -> {h}, \"Dimension\" -> Dim]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"16c59528-af06-4660-a550-7996c5171b07"],

Cell["\<\
ksAnsatz = DefineAnsatz[ksTheory,
  \"Density\" -> hbar (dt[h] - nuk lap[h] + tauk lap[lap[h]] - lamk/2 \
grad[h].grad[h]) - Dk hbar^2 - zetak grad[hbar].grad[hbar],
  \"Couplings\" -> {nuk, tauk, lamk, Dk, zetak}];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a77c91ff-02b3-4911-a5bd-3c0152774600"],

Cell["2. Symmetries, vertices, regulator", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"39febd54-d226-4c7c-b351-31803d21870b"],

Cell["\<\
The truncation keeps every monomial up to (i w)^2 (p^2)^2. Two of them are \
protected (their flow must vanish exactly), the four couplings get equations, \
and everything else is computed, reported and dropped. The regulators are \
those of Eq. 31, R^nu = D r_nu(q^2/k^2) and R^D = D r_D(q^2/k^2), with two \
independent shapes rn = r_nu and rd = r_D, as in the paper's Eqs. 34-37.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"84aafc52-8d51-4024-8cae-e31e4c856191"],

Cell["\<\
ksSymmetries = DeclareSymmetries[ksAnsatz,
  Protected[{hbar, h}, \"Frequency\" -> 1, \"Momentum\" -> 0, \"Reason\" -> \
\"Galilean Ward identity (the 1 in front of dt h)\"],
  Protected[{h, hbar}, \"Frequency\" -> 0, \"Momentum\" -> 0, \"Reason\" -> \
\"h -> h + c (shift symmetry)\"],
  Neglected[_, \"Reason\" -> \"beyond the simplest DE ansatz\"],
  NonRenormalised[lamk, \"Reason\" -> \"Galilean invariance\"],
  \"TruncationOrders\" -> <|\"Frequency\" -> 2, \"Momentum\" -> 2|>];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"28f2fa75-d73c-4be0-a5d8-193aec105ff6"],

Cell["ksVertices = BuildVertices[ksAnsatz];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"01eadc97-529d-4b67-afd6-5c2a5c5fc092"],

Cell["\<\
ksRegulator = DefineRegulator[ksAnsatz, {hbar, h} -> Dk q^2 rn[q^2/k^2], \
{hbar, hbar} -> -2 Dk rd[q^2/k^2], \"Shapes\" -> {rn, rd}];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"11f2125c-08a8-4dcb-8e58-c4a04cc1a3a7"],

Cell["\<\
3. Propagator, loops, projection, frequency integrals, closure, equations\
\>", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"dff07e49-3978-4c4e-b511-9eb73fb1c090"],

Cell["\<\
The projection card should show Projection.explicitVectors: the p^4 \
integrands are recomputed with explicit vectors and (Laplacian_p)^2 and \
compared. The closure card lists the two protected flows (exactly zero) and \
the four equations. The whole build takes about 20 s.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2c605c19-1a33-4742-b3fc-35f8d0ebbbb6"],

Cell["ksPropagator = BuildPropagator[ksVertices, ksRegulator];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"cbecdc6b-9519-4ba0-82ba-dedb2253a333"],

Cell["\<\
ksLoops = BuildLoopIntegrands[ksVertices, ksPropagator, ksRegulator];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b8b90ad4-aa8d-4fa6-afd6-594b68f53c76"],

Cell["ksProjected = ProjectOnMonomials[ksLoops, ksSymmetries];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"fa99f561-4cb4-4368-9d67-ee6b8a5f5041"],

Cell["ksIntegrated = IntegrateFrequencies[ksProjected];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"fe0160e2-bc07-484b-8cb0-148146f19b9c"],

Cell["ksClosure = CheckClosure[ksIntegrated, ksSymmetries];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"cc9ae37f-b5e7-4206-bdb1-35f263008a2c"],

Cell["ksEquations = AssembleEquations[ksClosure];", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9244e87a-c780-416b-affc-64c07e2e2855"],

Cell["ShowEquation[ksEquations, tauk]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6f4116d8-bd04-42bb-819b-a8cc3dde525c"],

Cell["4. The paper's flows (Eqs. 34-37)", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"fc3271de-8866-421a-993a-b1c99b6ebad8"],

Cell["\<\
Reference.wl transcribes Eqs. 34-37 (checked against the authors' notebook). \
Each is the integrand J of Int dqh/(2 Pi) over the real line, qh = q/k, in \
the paper's dimensionless variables Gh = lam^2/(k D^2), Fh = nu/D, Ch = k^2 \
zeta/D, Th = k^2 tau/D and eta = -d_s D/D, with x = qh^2, hn = Fh + Th x + \
rnu(x), hd = 1 + Ch x + rD(x), sn = d_s R^nu/D, sd = d_s R^D/D. Below: the \
four integrands, as functions of x.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"72603a2f-ef68-4813-aeee-7c77003c97a5"],

Cell["\<\
Get[FileNameJoin[{ksDir, \"Reference.wl\"}]];
Column[Table[c -> TraditionalForm[ksRefTerms[x][c]], {c, {\"D\", \"nu\", \
\"zeta\", \"tau\"}}], Spacings -> 2]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"693f0e54-227d-4530-ab08-e3dccfd8bee4"],

Cell["5. Comparison", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"4221025b-ddea-4102-b6e4-99f692dbcace"],

Cell["\<\
Checks.wl rewrites the library's integrand of each equation in the paper's \
variables (q = k qh, nu = D Fh, tau = D Th/k^2, zeta = D Ch/k^2, lam^2 = Gh k \
D^2, d_s D = -eta D), with s_nu and s_D written through the shapes (R = D r \
gives s = -(eta r + 2 x r')). The difference library - paper must vanish \
identically. ExactlyZeroQ evaluates it at random rational values of every \
symbol and of every shape atom r(x), r'(x), r''(x), r'''(x) separately, so it \
is a pointwise test, not a test after integration.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1047e158-b94e-4b7d-ab2f-6ac7cb4d6026"],

Cell["\<\
Get[FileNameJoin[{ksDir, \"Checks.wl\"}]];
ksLibrary[\"tau\"] // Short[#, 6] &\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"d9768c51-6e0e-431b-8223-158a0be063df"],

Cell["\<\
Table[c -> ExactlyZeroQ[ksLibrary[c] - (ksRef[c] /. ksSRules)], {c, {\"D\", \
\"nu\", \"zeta\", \"tau\"}}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"f81109a1-da99-4a9f-b8a3-23cb8c19bf34"],

Cell["\<\
The r''' terms: the tau flow contains third derivatives of both shapes, which \
the paper notes cancel for r_nu = alpha r_D.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"018b88ac-49d8-48ae-adc6-077594fb9afe"],

Cell["\<\
{ksThirdDerivatives[ksLibrary[\"tau\"]], \
ksThirdDerivatives[ksLibrary[\"tau\"] /. ksAlphaRegulator]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"d6bae97b-edd8-4a8a-a461-eb09fdc1f5a2"],

Cell["\<\
Time reversal (Eqs. 19, 32): in d = 1, with r_nu = alpha r_D, the point nu = \
alpha D, tau = alpha zeta is preserved by the flow, d_s nu = alpha d_s D and \
d_s tau = alpha d_s zeta. The second identity compares the p^4 projection of \
Gamma(1,1) with the p^2 projection of Gamma(0,2). Both hold at every qh in d \
= 1 and both fail in d = 2.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d2f0e20b-9f7d-4426-984d-c61a83b0b5bf"],

Cell["\<\
Table[d -> {ExactlyZeroQ[(ksLibrary[\"nu\", d] - al ksLibrary[\"D\", d]) /. \
ksAlphaRegulator /. ksTimeReversal],
   ExactlyZeroQ[(ksLibrary[\"tau\", d] - al ksLibrary[\"zeta\", d]) /. \
ksAlphaRegulator /. ksTimeReversal]}, {d, {1, 2}}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"116c31e4-1404-40a3-93b9-1321e3855e8b"],

Cell["6. Summary", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"7ae1f488-0713-4303-9616-be7091f5790f"],

Cell["\<\
The same checks as benchmarks/KuramotoSivashinsky/run.wl and the suite hook \
tests/acceptance/KuramotoSivashinsky.wl. The last cell rebuilds the equations \
from Pipeline.wl (about 20 s, cards hidden) and checks that they are \
identical to those built above, so that this notebook and the scripted \
benchmark cannot drift apart.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5cf3960b-29c2-4031-9ad4-2cca580c0488"],

Cell["\<\
ksResults = ksChecks[];
ksSummary[ksResults]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6a3ca6f8-db11-4ef7-9b20-cc0f19b5a6fa"],

Cell["If[AllTrue[ksResults, #Pass &], \"all checks pass\", Abort[]]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b1af7b5b-5dfc-4875-8448-4e53facd261f"],

Cell["\<\
nbIntegrands = ksEquations[\"Equations\"][[All, \"Integrand\"]];
Block[{ksEquations, Print}, Get[FileNameJoin[{ksDir, \"Pipeline.wl\"}]]; \
If[ksEquations[\"Equations\"][[All, \"Integrand\"]] === nbIntegrands, \"same \
equations as Pipeline.wl\", Abort[]]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2a114562-e044-4b4d-8124-e3b0ed8305aa"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"14.3 for Linux x86 (64-bit) (July 8, 2025)",
StyleDefinitions->"Default.nb",
ExpressionUUID->"3a83ea4a-218d-4992-bfcd-d1626f0a616d"
]
(* End of Notebook Content *)

